#!/usr/bin/python3
"""hearth-coop: Couch Co-op (experimental). Split-screen for 2-4 players on one screen, each player with their own
controller and their own copy of the game, even for games that have no split-screen of their own (the idea behind
PartyDeck and Nucleus Co-op). The copies find each other over the local network inside this PC, like a LAN party.

  hearth-coop check                      what this system has (gamescope, labwc, bwrap, umu-run, Proton...)
  hearth-coop pads                       controllers you can hand to players
  hearth-coop wait-button [--timeout S] [--exclude id,...]
                                         waits for someone to press a face button; prints which controller it was
  hearth-coop games                      games that can be started in Couch Co-op, and why others can't
  hearth-coop profiles                   the player names for seats 1-4
  hearth-coop profile-set --seat N --name NAME
  hearth-coop gbe-fetch                  downloads the Steam emulator ahead of time (start does it when needed)
  hearth-coop start --game ID --players N --pads id1,id2[,...] [--layout auto|vertical|horizontal|grid]
                    [--width W --height H] [--keyboard-seat N] [--exe PATH] [--stagger S] [--dry-run]
  hearth-coop status                     the running session
  hearth-coop stop                       stops every copy cleanly and tidies up

Output: "progress: N text" lines (N = 0-100), then one JSON line. Problems: "error: <plain message>", exit 1.
Layouts: "vertical" = side by side (split by a vertical line), "horizontal" = one above the other, "grid" = 2x2.

How a session runs (see docs/COUCH-COOP.md):
  * a nested labwc fills the screen (gamescope in Game Mode shows one window: this one);
  * each player's copy runs in its own nested gamescope (-W/-H = the player's part of the screen), placed by
    labwc window rules matched on a per-player app id, with a per-launch fallback rule;
  * each copy runs in bubblewrap with a fresh /dev holding only that player's controller (evdev, js, hidraw),
    an empty /run/udev, a /tmp of its own (the nested gamescope's X server makes its socket folder there),
    SDL_JOYSTICK_DISABLE_UDEV=1, its own HOME (and Wine prefix), and the real game folder
    read-only; the game sees a per-player copy-on-write view of its folder (fuse-overlayfs, or a symlink view);
  * Steam games get the Goldberg Steam emulator (gbe_fork) in that per-player view only, set to LAN.
Never writes into the real game install or the real Steam folder.
"""
